2. Behaviour matches the MCP path; same-day semantics agree, incl. boundaries.
   PASS

   UI predicate:  internal/handlers/accounts/handlers.go:320-324
       ay, am, ad := a.Date(); by, bm, bd := b.Date()
       return ay == by && am == bm && ad == bd
   MCP predicate: internal/services/mcpsvc/ledger/anchor.go, func sameDay
       return a.Year()==b.Year() && a.Month()==b.Month() && a.Day()==b.Day()
   Both compare wall-clock Y/M/D, each value in its OWN location.

   Empirical cross-package comparison (scratch copy $SCRATCH/b2cmp; a table
   test compiled into package accounts calling sameCalendarDay and into
   package ledger calling sameDay, same 7 inputs, outputs diffed):
   cmd: go test -v -run TestZZPredUI  ./internal/handlers/accounts/
        go test -v -run TestZZPredMCP ./internal/services/mcpsvc/ledger/
        diff <(ui) <(mcp)
   out: IDENTICAL   (diff empty, exit 0)
        stored 2026-08-15T00:00:00Z                       -> true  (both)
        stored 2026-08-15T23:30:00Z (LATER time component)-> true  (both)
        stored 2026-08-15T00:00:00-05:00                  -> true  (both)
        stored 2026-08-15T23:00:00-05:00 (= 8/16 04:00Z)  -> true  (both)
        stored 2026-08-16T01:00:00+09:00 (= 8/15 16:00Z)  -> false (both)
        stored 2026-08-14T23:59:59Z                       -> false (both)
        stored 2026-08-16T00:00:01Z                       -> false (both)
   The two paths agree on every case, including the two zone-crossing cases
   where wall-clock day and UTC-instant day disagree.

   End-to-end through the real handler + JSON store (scratch $SCRATCH/b2cmp,
   TestZZBoundaryEndToEnd), POSTing anchor_date=2026-08-15:
     stored 2026-08-15T23:30:00Z          -> 1 anchor, Amount 4210.55
     stored 2026-08-15T23:00:00-05:00     -> 1 anchor, Amount 4210.55
     stored 2026-08-14T23:59:59Z          -> 2 anchors (correctly not merged)

   OBSERVATION (not a criterion failure): the doc comment at handlers.go:316
   says the anchors are compared "in a's location". The code compares each
   value in its own location, not both in a's. The MCP twin carries no such
   claim. Behaviour is correct and matches MCP; only the comment overstates.

