   :559  if accts[0].Anchors[0].Amount != 4210.55 { t.Fatalf(...) }
   :566-570 BalanceAt(...) != 4210.55 { t.Fatalf(...) }

   Proved the amount assertion is load-bearing, not decorative, by mutation B
   on a scratch copy ($SCRATCH/b2mutB): replace the same-day anchor by keeping
   the OLD one -- count stays exactly 1, wrong anchor survives.
   cmd: go test -v -run 'TestHandleAddAnchor_ReplacesSameDayAnchor|
        TestHandleAddAnchor_SameDayReplacementIsDeterministic' \
        ./internal/handlers/accounts/
   out: handlers_test.go:498: anchor amount = 1000, want 4210.55
        handlers_test.go:501: anchor note = "stale statement", want "corrected statement"
        handlers_test.go:514: BalanceAt = 1000, want 4210.55
        --- FAIL: TestHandleAddAnchor_ReplacesSameDayAnchor
        handlers_test.go:559: iteration 0: authoritative amount = 1000, want 4210.55 on every run
        --- FAIL: TestHandleAddAnchor_SameDayReplacementIsDeterministic
   Both fail on the amount, with the count correct. The assertion therefore
   depends on which anchor survives.

   HONEST CAVEAT on the 50-iteration loop itself: I measured whether
   sort.Slice actually reorders all-equal keys on this toolchain.
   cmd: go run $SCRATCH/sortchk/main.go
   out: n= 2 equal keys: 0/20000 runs reordered by sort.Slice
        n= 3 equal keys: 0/20000 runs reordered by sort.Slice
        n=13 equal keys: 0/20000 runs reordered by sort.Slice
        n=20 equal keys: 0/20000 runs reordered by sort.Slice
   So the loop's 50 repetitions are unlikely to expose sort.Slice's documented
   instability at these sizes; the loop adds little over a single run in
   practice. The brief's acceptance wording -- "the same sequence of adds
   produces the same authoritative anchor every run" -- is nevertheless
   satisfied, and criterion 3 as posed (assertion depends on the survivor, not
   the count) is demonstrated above. Recording this so the lead can judge
   whether the loop is worth its cost, not as a defect.

